<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kontagora Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1859 CE – 1901 CE (approx.) · capital Kontagora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A late emirate of the Sokoto Caliphate in western Nigeria, built by a grandson of Usman dan Fodio and notorious for large-scale slave raiding.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontagora">Kontagora</a> was founded around 1859 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umaru-nagwamatse">Umaru Nagwamatse</a>, a son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-atiku">Abubakar Atiku</a> and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>. Leaving Sokoto, he gathered followers and conquered lands among the Kamuku, Gbagyi and other peoples between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-river">Kaduna</a> rivers, establishing his capital at Kontagora.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nagwamatse styled himself Sarkin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> lived largely by war. Its armies raided surrounding communities for captives, who were sold or settled on estates, and much of the region was depopulated. The emirate's rulers owed allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> but acted with considerable independence. Under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-nagwamatse">Ibrahim Nagwamatse</a>, from 1880, the raiding reached its peak.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontagora">Kontagora</a> in 1901, and Ibrahim was captured in 1902 and later restored. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>, and its emir still reigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> State.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1859 CE – 1901 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.70%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1859 CE – 1901 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kontagora</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Umaru Nagwamatse, Ibrahim Nagwamatse</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa, Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Kontagora</dd></dl></aside></div>

